# Tooling unit tests and repository checks, one target per module: Bazel
# owns isolation and parallelism, so no target runs the complete suite or
# invokes ao check/tidy. Every unit target runs exactly one module through the
# shared standard-library entry ao.graph.bazel_unittest, which fails a target
# whose module collects no tests.
#
# Categories come from the per-module input audit (static import analysis plus
# an empirical trace of every module's real file opens and process spawns):
#
# - declared unit targets: the module's only inputs are its declared sources,
#   its imported support modules, and its library dependencies; they cache.
# - declared-data unit targets: like the above, plus a short explicit list of
#   repository files read through PROJECT_ROOT; the data labels keep the
#   cache key honest on POSIX symlink runfiles and provide the files in a
#   Windows copied tree.
# - host-backed unit targets: the audit observed host-process spawns, physical
#   git-state reads, or dynamic/whole-tree scans. "local" keeps them
#   unsandboxed and "external" forces a real run, so no cached result can
#   stand in for the checkout or tools they use. The runtime is the installed
#   native py_runtime, and host tool discovery uses the inherited PATH.
#
# Host-backed targets pass the declared VERSION.txt anchor as --live-checkout.
# On both operating systems the entry then always re-execs itself from the
# physical checkout: Windows copies runfiles, and even on POSIX the already
# loaded runfiles ao packages would shadow the physical checkout. The child
# receives the plain arguments without the flag, which is the loop guard; no
# loaded package is patched. Cacheable targets never pass the flag and run
# straight from their declared runfiles.
#
# The repository checks at the bottom read the live physical checkout through
# the declared VERSION.txt anchor resolved by the official rules_python
# runfiles library.

